### What are Stable Values?

Stable Values (JEP 502, preview in Java 24) provide a lazily initialized, at-most-once container that the JVM can treat almost like a `final` field for optimization. A `StableValue<T>` is created empty and set exactly once, typically via a Supplier on first read.

### Why does double-checked locking need replacement?

Double-checked locking (DCL) is the traditional pattern for thread-safe lazy initialization. It is verbose, error-prone, and easy to get wrong.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Map;

public class RateService {

    // WRONG: broken double-checked locking (missing volatile)
    private Map<String, BigDecimal> rates;

    public Map<String, BigDecimal> getRates() {
        if (rates == null) {                    // check 1
            synchronized (this) {
                if (rates == null) {            // check 2
                    rates = loadRatesFromDb();  // expensive
                }
            }
        }
        return rates;
    }
    // Bug: without volatile, Thread B may see a partially constructed Map!

    // CORRECT but verbose: proper DCL
    private volatile Map<String, BigDecimal> ratesFixed;

    public Map<String, BigDecimal> getRatesFixed() {
        Map<String, BigDecimal> local = ratesFixed;  // read volatile once
        if (local == null) {
            synchronized (this) {
                local = ratesFixed;
                if (local == null) {
                    ratesFixed = local = loadRatesFromDb();
                }
            }
        }
        return local;
    }
    // Correct, but 12 lines for lazy init!
}
```

### How do Stable Values simplify this?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.lang.StableValue;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Map;

public class RateService {

    private final StableValue<Map<String, BigDecimal>> rates = StableValue.of();

    public Map<String, BigDecimal> getRates() {
        return rates.orElseSet(this::loadRatesFromDb);
        // First call: runs loadRatesFromDb(), stores result
        // All subsequent calls: returns cached result instantly
        // Thread-safe: supplier runs at most once
    }
}
```

```text
Thread A calls getRates() → loadRatesFromDb() executes → result cached
Thread B calls getRates() → returns cached result (no loading)
Thread C calls getRates() concurrently with A → waits, then returns A's result
```

### How is StableValue different from other lazy patterns?

| Pattern | Thread-safe | Correct | Lines | JVM-optimizable |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Double-checked locking | If volatile | Error-prone | 12 | No |
| Lazy holder idiom | Yes | Yes | 8 | No |
| StableValue | Yes | By design | 2 | Yes (constant fold) |
| AtomicReference | Race possible | Supplier may run twice | 3 | No |

### What are the rules for using StableValue?

- Create with `StableValue.of()` and initialize with `orElseSet(Supplier)`.
- The Supplier runs at most once, even under concurrent access.
- After initialization, reads are as fast as reading a final field.
- Do not call `orElseSet` with different Suppliers on the same StableValue (the first one wins).

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: trying to set the value twice
StableValue<String> name = StableValue.of();
name.orElseSet(() -> "Rajesh");
name.orElseSet(() -> "Priya");  // Returns "Rajesh" (first set wins)
// This is NOT an error, but the second supplier is never called

// WRONG: using StableValue for mutable state
StableValue<List<String>> list = StableValue.of();
list.orElseSet(ArrayList::new).add("item");  // Mutating the value defeats the purpose
// StableValue is for immutable lazy initialization
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- StableValue is preview in Java 24. The API may change before finalization.
- The JVM can constant-fold stable values: after initialization, the JIT may inline the value directly at call sites.
- `StableValue.ofList(size, indexMapper)` creates a lazily-initialized list where each element is computed on first access.
- For per-enum-constant caches, `StableValue` is cleaner than `EnumMap` with lazy computation.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What happens if the Supplier throws? (The StableValue remains unset. The next call to `orElseSet` will try again.)
- Is StableValue the same as AtomicReference.compareAndSet? (No. AtomicReference allows the Supplier to run multiple times under contention. StableValue guarantees at-most-once.)
- When is double-checked locking still appropriate? (On JDKs before 24, or when you cannot use preview features.)
